{"format":1,"seq":585,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I'm starting PR B, stacked on top of A, branching issue-383-workflows from the current HEAD. I'm noting that since this PR's functions run in a live Pit lacking the new globals, the workflow-resources test type-checks against the new repo contract, but actual usage requires PR A merged first, which the stacking ensures. I'm designing the changes to waitForRun to map completion outcomes based on conclusion.\n\nOn timeout behavior, I'm deciding it should never throw but instead return a pending-style result with status, attempt info, and a hint to retry, while raising errors only applies to unsuccessful completions. For typing, since the parsed JSON run object is loosely typed as `any`, I'm casting it to a proper shape with status, conclusion, url, and jobs so the conclusion field is cleanly typed as string or null, fixing the awkward typing issue flagged in the earlier GitHub discussion where users accessed `.conclusion` directly on results.\n\nFor the commit-matching function, I'm structuring the return to include outcome, conclusion, match, and run, spreading in a hint field only when the outcome is pending so the type stays optional rather than always present.\n\nFor waitForChecks, on timeout I want it to always return a pending-style object with notes, hints, and elapsed time regardless of the raise setting, and I'm removing \"timed_out\" as a separate outcome value since pending now covers that case. I also need to update the corresponding test files for workflow waiting and PR checks, plus adjust documentation text around timeout behavior and bounds. Then I move on to writing the JSDoc for the pull-request-opening function, describing how it creates or switches branches, commits files, pushes, and opens the PR.\n\nNow I'm working out the branch validation logic, checking the current branch against the target, creating or switching branches as needed, committing with push enabled, and then checking for an existing open PR on that branch via a list query.\n\nIf no PR exists, I create one and parse the PR number from the returned URL, returning branch, sha, url, created flag, and number. I'm noting that pushing already triggers a check for merged/closed PRs, and that if the commit fails post-switch, the branch stays switched — errors should just propagate naturally rather than attempting rollback.\n\nNow I'm sketching the merge function, starting with input validation and fetching the PR's current state, base branch, head branch, and URL via the view command.\n\nI'm checking the repo's commit history to figure out what merge method they actually use, since commit subjects lack PR number suffixes, which hints at rebase or merge rather than squash. I'm leaning toward making `method` a required input field rather than assuming a default, and setting `deleteBranch` to default false matching gh's own default behavior.\n\nI'm also planning a sync step after merging — switching to the base branch and pulling with `--ff-only`, then returning the merge result along with sync details for the branch and head.\n\nDeciding against exposing `sync` with `auto` merge since queued auto-merges don't make sense to sync immediately afterward — I'll skip exposing `auto` entirely and just keep `method`, `deleteBranch`, `sync`, and `repo` as the surface. I'm also noting that gh's `--delete-branch` flag already switches the local branch to default and may handle cleanup, so my own switch/pull logic should just be a safe no-op in that case. I need to verify the exact gh command arguments for deleteBranch, and I'm planning tests following the existing workflow test patterns.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_bdrk_01BJtaRDVwCBY8RdcQxNViXW","name":"typescript","arguments":{"code":"// @pit: {\"label\": \"Branch PR B and inspect resources test and wait tests\"}\nasync ({ shell: { execFile }, workspa